# Findings already raised with whoever can fix them. They stay in every report;
# they simply stop setting the "needs attention" exit code, so a scheduled run
# is quiet until something NEW goes wrong.
#
# An alert that fires every night gets muted, and then it costs you the one that
# matters. This file is what keeps that from happening — and it is a record of
# "we know, it is being handled", not a place to hide findings.
#
# Copy to acknowledged.txt and edit. Two kinds of entry, and they do not
# collide:
#
#   1. A hostname          — that device's collection failure is known
#   2. A finding key       — that specific policy finding is known
#
# Finding keys are printed by `netconfig-mirror audit` when it exits non-zero;
# copy them from there rather than typing them.

# --- Devices storing a 65-byte error string instead of a configuration.
# The collector reports these as successfully collected.
# Raised with the collector team 2026-09-12.
UK-LD5-R316-RTR1
UK-LD5-R316-RTR2

# --- Routing policy findings.
# Uncomment the ones that have been raised. Leaving a finding here that nobody
# is actually working on is worse than not having the file at all.
#
# dangling:<hostname>:<what refers>:<name it refers to>
#
# dangling:HK1-L3-SW:route-map static-into-bgp:redistribute-static-into-bgp
